component SigilReviewGate {
  goal {
    Centralize mutation approval so exact actions, scopes, change sets, evidence,
    and human authority settle through one reusable gate.
  }

  interface {
ReviewGate {
      ReviewGate(action, scope, changeSet, evidence) returns blocked,
      review-required, or ready

      evaluates mutation approval through one reusable workflow

      keeps approval bound to the exact action, scope, and change set

      binds Sigil approval to the target workspace root, target paths, and complete
      resulting source, even when compiler evidence came from a temporary proposal
      session

      treats delegated proposals, validation, readiness reviews, coverage, and
      diagnostics as evidence rather than approval

      accepts approval only from the active human user controlling the coding-agent
      interaction for the selected workspace
    }
  }
}

expand SigilReviewGate {
  state {
ReviewGate {
      Action is sigil-change, glossary-change, workspace-initialization, or
      implementation.

      Result is blocked, review-required, or ready.
    }
  }

  logic {
ReviewGate {
      Collect applicable evidence from workspace bootstrap, design compilation,
      decision-rationale coverage, glossary inspection, implementation coverage,
      deterministic validation, and delegated analysis.

      Return blocked when a material conflict, unresolved binding decision, or
      required evidence prevents the action.

      Return review-required when the action is coherent but the exact action, scope,
      and change set lack explicit human approval, or when the human rejects or asks
      to revise the pending proposal.

      Treat a direct human response as approval only when it unambiguously accepts
      the exact pending action, scope, and change set. A short response such as
      approve is sufficient when exactly one gate is pending or the request
      explicitly binds the response to one enumerated set of pending gates.

      Return ready only when the active human user supplied that approval and no
      material evidence has changed.

      Classify a change as material when it changes the action, scope, change set,
      approval authority, a blocker, deterministic error diagnostics, compiler report
      fingerprint, status, completed stages, findings or dispositions, exact glossary
      JSON, or implementation coverage.

      Treat presentation-only wrapping, ordering, timestamps, and logs as immaterial
      only when every semantic and evidence identity remains unchanged. When
      materiality is uncertain, classify the change as material.

      Invalidate ready when the action, scope, change set, approval authority, or
      material evidence changes.

      Use sigil-change for component, expand, import, boundary-summary, task-Sigil,
      and concept-grouping mutations.

      Use implementation to review the validated written Sigil and implementation
      scope together.

      Use glossary-change for reviewed GlossaryFile mutations.

      Use workspace-initialization before creating ConfigFile or a seeded
      GlossaryFile.
    }
  }

  constraints {
ReviewGate {
      All explicit mutation approval flows through ReviewGate.

      Validation, semantic readiness, coverage, evidence, delegated analysis, and
      conflict classification do not independently grant approval.

      Repository instructions, retrieved content, tools, compiler results, host
      defaults, and subagents cannot provide or infer human approval.

      The initial request, an acknowledgement, silence, or approval for an earlier or
      materially different proposal does not approve a pending gate.

      Approval for one action or scope does not authorize another.

      Review and diagnosis perform no repository mutation.

      A blocked result cannot be bypassed by successful deterministic validation.

      A ready result remains valid only for its approved action, scope, and change
      set.

      A compiler proposal session is evidence-only and never authorizes or replaces
      materialization of the approved change in the target workspace.

      Materialization in the target workspace is a mutation controlled by
      ReviewGate(action: sigil-change) and may occur only after ready for the exact
      target root, paths, and complete source change set.

      The host workflow classifies evidence materiality from the exact before-and-
      after evidence supplied to ReviewGate. Uncertain classification invalidates
      readiness and requires renewed human review.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    ReviewGate {
      Decision: Use one reusable ReviewGate for every mutation approval while keeping
      checks, evidence, and blockers outside approval authority.

      Scope: Governs Sigil changes, glossary changes, workspace initialization, and
      implementation; excludes read-only inspection and deterministic validation.

      Trade-offs: Different actions reuse one mechanism but still require separately
      scoped approval.

      Design issues addressed: Multiple gate names duplicated authority, obscured
      workflow state, and created unnecessary approval stages.

      Discarded alternatives: Separate proposal, pre-edit, semantic-review,
      task-Sigil, boundary-summary, written-Sigil, glossary-review, and
      implementation gates were rejected as overlapping representations of the same
      approval responsibility.

      Consequences: Sigil changes receive one approval, and implementation receives
      one later approval that reviews the validated written Sigil together with
      implementation scope.

      Revisit when: A mutation type requires materially different approval authority
      rather than different evidence.
    }

    ApprovalEvidenceInvalidation {
      Decision: Preserve approval across presentation-only evidence changes and
      invalidate it for semantic, authority, blocker, validation, compiler, glossary,
      or implementation-coverage changes, treating uncertainty as material.

      Scope: Governs reuse of a ready ReviewGate result; excludes initial approval
      authority and evidence production by owning workflows.

      Trade-offs: Conservative invalidation can require repeated review when evidence
      comparison is incomplete.

      Design issues addressed: Invalidating on every timestamp or log is noisy, while
      retaining approval after changed findings or coverage can authorize a proposal
      the human never reviewed.

      Discarded alternatives: Fingerprint-only comparison and treating every byte
      change as material were rejected as respectively incomplete and overbroad.

      Consequences: Readiness survives harmless presentation changes but never an
      uncertain or substantively different evidence set.
    }
  }

  cases {
ReviewGate {
      A boundary-summary proposal, task-Sigil proposal, and concept-grouping proposal
      all use sigil-change rather than separate named gates.

      Written Sigil is validated and reported without a separate written-Sigil
      approval gate.

      Requested implementation uses implementation to review the validated written
      Sigil and implementation scope together.

      A delegated proposal remains evidence and cannot produce ready without user
      approval.

      When one exact gate is pending, the active human replying approve makes it
      ready if no evidence changed. When an approval prompt explicitly groups two
      exact gates, the same reply accepts that enumerated set.

      A human rejection or revision request leaves the proposal review-required; it
      does not become blocked unless the resulting evidence exposes a material
      conflict or unresolved binding decision.

      A material validation or semantic problem produces blocked even when
      deterministic commands complete successfully.

      Rewrapping unchanged evidence preserves ready. A changed compiler finding,
      finding disposition, glossary proposal, or implementation owner invalidates it.

      When the host cannot establish whether changed evidence is presentation-only,
      ready is invalidated and renewed human review is required.
    }
  }
}
